# arch/os/macos/io — the system call, without a C library to make it through.
#
# Everything in here exists to not be a call into libSystem, and the compiler is
# entitled to make one anyway: a byte loop becomes memcpy(), an array makes the
# stack protector emit __stack_chk_fail. A build where the line below prints
# anything at all has grown a dependency this directory exists to not have:
#
#	nm -u obj/arch/os/macos/io/io.o
#
# There is no nostdlib.o beside io.o the way there is under arch/os/linux. That
# file is the six data symbols an ELF -nostdlib link leaves undefined, and two
# of them are stdout and stderr — which on macOS are not data symbols at all but
# macros over __stdoutp, so there is nothing here for such a file to define. The
# program that needed it, tools/ub, is an ELF shape besides (see the platform
# README), so on this platform nothing links without libSystem in the first
# place.
obj-$(CONFIG_OS_IO) += io.o

# -fno-builtin, and -fno-stack-protector for the same reason one step further
# out: a toolchain that turns the stack protector on by default puts a call to
# the library's __stack_chk_fail in every function that has an array in it,
# which in here is most of them.
ccflags-y += -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector
